<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kayan people (Borneo)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kayan Dayak</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kayan are an Indigenous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dayak-people">Dayak people</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>, living along the upper reaches of rivers such as the Kayan in North Kalimantan, the Mahakam in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-kalimantan">East Kalimantan</a> and the Baram and Rajang in Sarawak. They were traditionally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farmers living in longhouses under a hereditary aristocracy, and were renowned as warriors and craftsmen. A Kayan chiefdom was the origin of the ruling house of the Bulungan Sultanate.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kayan-people-borneo.jpg" alt="Kayan people (Borneo)" width="320" height="469" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: W. H. Furness 3rd · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kajandajakfrau.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
